"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 2.2: A visual search system=E2=80=99s i=
nput-output</figcaption></div></figure></center>
<h4 id=3D"choosing-the-right-ml-category">Choosing the right ML category</h=
4>
<p>The output of the model is a set of ranked images that are similar to th=
e query image. As a result, visual search systems can be framed as a rankin=
g problem. In general, the goal of ranking problems is to rank a collection=
 of items, such as images, websites, products, etc., based on their relevan=
ce to a query, so that more relevant items appear higher in the search resu=
lts. Many ML applications, such as recommendation systems, search engines, =
document retrieval, and online advertising, can be framed as ranking proble=
ms. In this chapter, we will use a widely-used approach called representati=
on learning. Let=E2=80=99s examine this in more detail.</p>
<p><strong>Representation learning.</strong> In representation learning [3]=
, a model is trained to transform input data, such as images, into represen=
tations called embeddings. Another way of describing this is that the model=
 maps input images to points in an N-dimensional space called embedding spa=
ce. These embeddings are learned so that similar images have embeddings tha=
t are in close proximity to each other, in the space. Figure 2.3 illustrate=
s how two similar images are mapped onto two points in close proximity with=
in the embedding space. To demonstrate, we visualize image embeddings (deno=
ted by =E2=80=98<span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xm=
