<p>To achieve this goal, we need to use a loss function to measure the qual=
ity of the produced embeddings. Different loss functions are designed for c=
ontrastive training, and interviewers don=E2=80=99t usually expect you to h=
old an in-depth discussion. However, it is crucial to have a high-level und=
erstanding of how contrastive loss functions work.</p>
<p>We are going to briefly discuss how a simplified contrastive loss operat=
es. If you are interested in learning more about contrastive losses, refer =
to [9].</p>
