/span></span> is the total number of points and <span class=3D"katex"><span=
 class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">=
<semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex=
">D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-h=
idden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: =
0.6833em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.=
02778em;">D</span></span></span></span> is the point dimension.</p>
<p>In a large-scale system in which <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"ka=
tex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><=
mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">N</annotat=
ion></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"tru=
e"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.6833em;"><=
/span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.10903em;">N<=
/span></span></span></span> may easily run into the billions, the linear ti=
me complexity is too slow.</p>
<h5 id=3D"approximate-nearest-neighbor-ann">Approximate nearest neighbor (A=
NN)</h5>
<p>In many applications, showing users similar enough items is sufficient, =
and there is no need to perform an exact nearest neighbor search.</p>
<p>In ANN algorithms, a particular data structure is used to reduce the tim=
e complexity of NN search to sublinear (e.g., <span class=3D"katex"><span c=
lass=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><s=
emantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>=C3=97<=
/mo><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></=
mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(D \times logN)</annotatio=
n></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"=
><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-=
align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-rig=
ht: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord m=
athnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">D</span><span class=3D"mspace=
" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">=C3=97</spa=
n><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><s=
pan class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-ali=
gn: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:=
 0.01968em;">l</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">o</span><span class=3D=
"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.03588em;">g</span><span class=3D=
"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.10903em;">N</span><span class=3D=
"mclose">)</span></span></span></span>. They usually require up-front prepr=
ocessing or additional space.</p>
<p>ANN algorithms can be divided into the following three categories:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tree-based ANN</li>
<li>Locality-sensitive hashing (LSH)-based ANN</li>
<li>Clustering-based ANN</li>
</ul>
<p>There are various algorithms within each category, and interviewers typi=
cally do not expect you to know every detail. It=E2=80=99s adequate to have=
 a high-level understanding of them. So, let=E2=80=99s briefly cover each c=
ategory.</p>
<h5 id=3D"tree-based-ann">Tree-based ANN</h5>
<p>Tree-based algorithms form a tree by splitting the space into multiple p=
artitions. Then, they leverage the characteristics of the tree to perform a=
 faster search.</p>
<p>We form the tree by iteratively adding new criteria to each node. For in=
stance, one criterion for the root node can be: gender <span class=3D"katex=
"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/M=
athML"><semantics><mrow><mo>=3D</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"applicat=
ion/x-tex">=3D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-h=
tml" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=
=3D"height: 0.3669em;"></span><span class=3D"mrel">=3D</span></span></span>=
</span> male. This means any point with a female attribute belongs to the l=
eft sub-tree.</p>
